#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171642 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5256297-c928-54e3-bf4d-35f5734f5632" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"}] :question/id #uuid "38dc8ce4-246d-54bd-a400-48c801d9387f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12344" :question/problem "$AAAAAAA$\nConsider the surface integral of the function\n$f(x,y,z) = z-y$\nover the parametric surface\n$\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u^2, \\: -v, \\: v \\big\\rangle$\nfor $(u,v) \\in D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, where $u \\geq 0$. What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| = 2\\sqrt{2}u$.*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D$, the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\| \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| = 2\\sqrt{2}u\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(u, v)) &= (z - y) \\mid _{x = u^{2}y =-vz = v} \\\\\n&= v - (-v) \\\\\n&= 2v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)f(x, y, z)dS &= ∬_(D)f(r(u, v))‖r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}‖dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)4 2 u vdA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171643 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b529430-3406-506c-b67d-c067dfee44d5" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"}] :question/id #uuid "fce43073-3f48-5e12-a5c0-ecef3a31a57a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12346" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n$\\iint\\limits_{S} (x-y)\\: \\textrm{d}S$,\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n$x = u+v, \\qquad y = u-v, \\qquad z = v$\nfor $0 \\leq u \\leq 3$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 3$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D$, the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\| \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u+v, \\: u-v, \\: v\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u}, \\frac{∂y}{∂u}, \\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u + v), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(u - v), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 1, 0⟩ \\\\\nr_{v}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v}, \\frac{∂y}{∂v}, \\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u + v), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(u - v), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1,-1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′} &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}, -\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1,-1,-2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}‖ &= ‖⟨1,-1,-2⟩‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1^{2} + (-1)^{2} + (-2)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 1 + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(u, v)) &= x - y \\mid _{x = u + vy = u - vz = v} \\\\\n&= (u + v) - (u - v) \\\\\n&= 2v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote\n\n$$\nD = \\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 3, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 3\\}\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)(x - y)dS &= ∬_(D)f(r(u, v))‖r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}‖dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)2v\\sqrt{6}dA \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{6}∬_(D)2vdA \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{6}∫_{0}^{3}2vdv \\cdot ∫_{0}^{3}1du \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{6} \\cdot [v^{2}]_{0}^{3} \\cdot [u]_{0}^{3} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{6} \\cdot 9 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 27\\sqrt{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5256297-c928-54e3-bf4d-35f5734f5632" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9be70133-e143-555a-b316-c857cb78e325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u^{2}v^{2}\\sqrt{u^{2} + v^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169046} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c62bb4-f2b7-5a28-bab7-5df3a9504149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv\\sqrt{u^{2} + v^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169047} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf2f94a-1a8b-5d99-8a70-f07f6c82f8fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u^{2}v^{2}\\sqrt{uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169048} {:answer/id #uuid "0966e2fa-eeb2-5012-9af2-d57f5e7b0e1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u^{2}v^{2}\\sqrt{u + v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169049} {:answer/id #uuid "2e1bec9b-3572-5c9b-a9e1-b8bf0036f7d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv\\sqrt{uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9be70133-e143-555a-b316-c857cb78e325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u^{2}v^{2}\\sqrt{u^{2} + v^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169046} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b5413c5-cd70-5f06-8ea5-ec7c6d11e516" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169045}] :question/id #uuid "25e44fde-444d-5880-ba2e-43dd4bc039db" :question/math-academy-id "q-160711" :question/problem "$∬_(S)f(x, y, z)dS = ∬_(D)AAAAAAAdA$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function\n$f(x, y, z) = xyz$\nover the parametric surface\n$r(u, v) = ⟨u, v, uv⟩$\nfor $(u, v) ∈ D⊆R^{2}$. What is the missing expression?"}]}
